adjudication.rationale:
In the new `AccessTokenContext` constructor, the null checks are:
```java
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null grantType not allowed");
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");
```
The second call mistakenly re-validates `grantType` instead of `rawTokenId`, so `rawTokenId` can be null without triggering a fail-fast exception (despite the message claiming otherwise). This is a real correctness defect introduced by the diff; `encodeTokenId()` later concatenates `':' + tokenContext.getRawTokenId()`, which will yield IDs ending with `:null` if `rawTokenId` is null.
